## Competitor: 思考の深淵 (Japanese long-form reference)

- Duration: strongest observed uploads are roughly 35–45 minutes, with some extending toward 55 minutes. Length is earned by successive revelations, not one explanation repeated.
- Title: a long Japanese identity/pain contradiction followed by a theory or psychological lens. Borrow the promise shape, never wording or unsupported theory.
- Opening: begin with a sensory, recognizable tension, then make the viewer's identity conflict visible before opening the central psychological question.
- Script rhythm: symbolic observation → source-bounded mechanism → implication → return of the same symbol with changed meaning. Scenes never serve as evidence or chronological plot.
- Research: theory is compact and precise. It illuminates a lived pattern rather than becoming an academic lecture.
- Ending: return once to the central image, replace harsh self-judgment with a more exact observation, then stop quietly.
- Thumbnail: off-white paper field, high-contrast black ink silhouette/line work, one controlled gold accent and oversized gold Japanese headline with heavy black outline. One symbolic conflict, no collage or hard split.
- Japan localization: use ordinary Japanese rooms, objects and emotional wording. Do not import spiritual certainty, trauma claims or foreign identity labels.


- Use a sensory opening and one identity tension; pivot to psychology within one or two sentences so imagery never becomes scene-first storytelling.
- Hold one core question, one or two source-backed mechanisms, and a revelation ladder of three to five genuinely different implications.
- Return an ordinary object two to four times only when its meaning advances: recognition → mechanism → implication → closing image.
- Use a vignette as an editorial illustration, never as an anecdote, case study or proof of a claim.
- Each movement must reveal a new fact, implication or self-understanding. Do not inflate duration with disclaimers, tips or generic reassurance.
- End with a reflective landing rather than a recap, motivational speech or extended CTA.
