  "environment_bible": "Use sparse ordinary Japanese domestic environments rendered as symbolic ink spaces: a kitchen or hallway corner, a low storage cabinet, a snack drawer, a muted dining area, or an uncluttered room with one meaningful object. Keep architecture understated and partly abstracted so the psychological process remains central. The recurring half-open drawer is the principal visual anchor: dark interior, slightly raised front panel, visible tension in its handle, and enough negative space around it for repeated approaches or shadow echoes. The drawer may transform editorially into a threshold, revolving doorway, tunnel, or black aperture, but its recognizable household origin must remain apparent. Show temptation through object placement, shadows, hand-shaped brush traces, repeated opening marks, or a small snack wrapper; never use readable packaging text. Use off-white paper as the base, charcoal black for conflict and repetition, muted slate-blue and khaki to preserve mascot continuity, and a single small gold #FFD700 accent at a turning point. Visualize habit and situational cues through repeated spatial arrangements, paths, recurring light shapes, and environmental consistency—not through written diagrams or labels. All surfaces, screens, papers, books, packages, signs, and objects must remain text-free and free of logos. Preserve one dominant symbol per composition, generous negative space, and continuity of object position when cutting between shots.",
  "opening_visual_contract": {
