  "character_bible": "Recurring mascot: a bald round-headed male cartoon figure with a large spherical bald head, pale cream skin, heavy sleepy-sad eyelids with one crease above each eye, round black eyes with tiny white highlights, thin curved black eyebrows, a minimal curved-arc nose, and a small neutral line mouth. Chibi proportions with a compact, slightly chubby torso. Outfit remains unchanged: muted slate-blue crewneck sweatshirt over a white collared dress shirt, khaki straight-leg trousers, and blue canvas sneakers with white laces. Use a thick, slightly irregular black outline and the exact face, colors, proportions, and outfit established in the thumbnail. His emotional range is restrained and adult: quiet fatigue, hesitation, self-monitoring, mild shame, confusion, relief, and calm attention; never screaming, melodramatic, heroic, or comic. His posture communicates inner conflict through lowered shoulders, paused hands, repeated backward glances, rigid stillness, or gradual relaxed movement. Keep the mascot visually identical across all shots. When contrasting him with a person who appears not to struggle, use only an anonymous simplified silhouette without facial detail or distinctive identity. No hair, beard, glasses, accessories, alternate clothing, gender changes, or second mascot.",
  "environment_bible": "Use sparse ordinary Japanese domestic environments rendered as symbolic ink spaces: a kitchen or hallway corner, a low storage cabinet, a snack drawer, a muted dining area, or an uncluttered room with one meaningful object. Keep architecture understated and partly abstracted so the psychological process remains central. The recurring half-open drawer is the principal visual anchor: dark interior, slightly raised front panel, visible tension in its handle, and enough negative space around it for repeated approaches or shadow echoes. The drawer may transform editorially into a threshold, revolving doorway, tunnel, or black aperture, but its recognizable household origin must remain apparent. Show temptation through object placement, shadows, hand-shaped brush traces, repeated opening marks, or a small snack wrapper; never use readable packaging text. Use off-white paper as the base, charcoal black for conflict and repetition, muted slate-blue and khaki to preserve mascot continuity, and a single small gold #FFD700 accent at a turning point. Visualize habit and situational cues through repeated spatial arrangements, paths, recurring light shapes, and environmental consistency—not through written diagrams or labels. All surfaces, screens, papers, books, packages, signs, and objects must remain text-free and free of logos. Preserve one dominant symbol per composition, generous negative space, and continuity of object position when cutting between shots.",
  "opening_visual_contract": {
    "thumbnail_scene": "The thumbnail establishes the emotional contradiction: repeated restraint around a half-open snack drawer has made the mascot visibly exhausted, while the drawer itself feels like a recurring source of conflict. The opening must pay off that promise by showing not merely a person resisting food, but the cost of repeatedly encountering the same cue and treating each encounter as a test of character. Preserve the half-open drawer, the mascot identity, the sparse ink language, and the single gold accent, but do not recreate the thumbnail composition literally.",
    "first_frame_scene": "Full-bleed off-white paper tableau in the same ink style. Begin close to the dark seam of a closed household drawer; the seam slowly appears to breathe open by a few centimeters. Instead of showing the mascot immediately, reveal several overlapping black brush impressions of the same hand reaching toward the handle and stopping just before contact, as if multiple moments occupy one frame. The recurring mascot is reflected only as a simplified distorted shadow on the cabinet surface, not as a second character. A tiny gold #FFD700 snack wrapper edge is visible inside the opening. No text, labels, packaging words, or explanatory graphics. The image should immediately suggest that the central problem is repeated contact with a cue, not one dramatic act of temptation.",
    "first_15s_visuals": [
      "0-3 seconds: Hold on the almost-closed drawer seam. A faint black brush tremor gathers around the handle while the paper texture remains visible. The drawer opens a fraction, then stops. No mascot face yet; the withheld reveal creates recognition and tension.",
      "3-6 seconds: Cut to a side view of the recurring mascot entering the sparse domestic space. He notices the same drawer, pauses, and lowers his hand before touching it. Keep his sleepy-sad face and slate-blue, cream, khaki, and blue outfit exactly consistent with the thumbnail.",
      "6-10 seconds: Without changing the room, show three translucent ink echoes of his earlier approach around the drawer. Each echo ends at the same stopping point. The echoes are editorial symbols of repeated encounters, not literal ghosts or evidence. Keep the composition singular and uncluttered.",
      "10-13 seconds: The mascot steps away, but the drawer remains half-open behind him. The dark opening briefly expands into a soft circular ink aperture, then returns to the ordinary drawer shape, establishing the environment as a recurring cue.",
      "13-15 seconds: The mascot stops at the edge of frame and looks back. The drawer stays still; the gold wrapper catches one restrained glint. End on the contrast between his visible effort and the unremarkable persistence of the situation, with no text or graphic overlay."
    ]
  }
}
